#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"}] :question/id #uuid "a663f68e-1fb9-5653-a128-1b55c2a58769" :question/math-academy-id "e-3449" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx,$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124270 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "7297f213-2db2-5da2-9bba-cac7e31f6b64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3450" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\n- $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)\n$$\n\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,1) & =(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1+1)+\\frac{(1)(1)}{1+1+1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124366 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "97192aac-6c1a-5ead-962b-0bf7ab573661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6109" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = -\\dfrac{2}{y},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the partial derivatives of a function exist where the original function is defined and the partial derivatives are defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y^2}\\right)$ is defined only when $\\dfrac{x}{y^2}>0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x > 0, \\: y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following:\n\n- The partial derivative $f_x(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is defined only when $x \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_x$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_x} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\n- The partial derivative $f_y(x,y) =  -\\dfrac{2}{y}$ is defined only when $y \\neq 0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is \n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, y \\neq 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07926aa9-5343-5729-9186-d16cbe2aedd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266209} {:answer/id #uuid "42a13c27-a866-577a-a101-0920417c8ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266739} {:answer/id #uuid "50ec6ab6-c2b6-55db-b1f7-eb5fe2c022af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z>0\\,\\text{and}\\,z\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266867} {:answer/id #uuid "d88f018f-6825-5ac2-b074-e1f757535e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-z\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,z>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268064} {:answer/id #uuid "d9318d3f-d1ac-5d94-a529-e27fcf6b8161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,z>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42a13c27-a866-577a-a101-0920417c8ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266739} :answer-field/id #uuid "6646ac7a-fca3-5840-8087-cc1aa2c3adf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a263e5dc-ce78-5c3c-8cfc-58a5ae184325" :question/math-academy-id "q-132643" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}},$ find the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ exists." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x-y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{(x-y)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}\\cap {(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,x-y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e7d715-580d-5725-8214-f78ece3f5eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0,\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266510} {:answer/id #uuid "2c39e45d-4896-59fc-bfe8-43d93450b95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266537} {:answer/id #uuid "662136c8-664f-5c2b-9822-b2984e8ad6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267050} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c5444b-2701-54f3-8aa3-c154d90ad878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268178} {:answer/id #uuid "fec00bbc-ad9c-5e12-9e7f-4b3134df6e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662136c8-664f-5c2b-9822-b2984e8ad6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267050} :answer-field/id #uuid "6534fcd6-db88-5988-8f90-35cfff7b41e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a66c3ca8-3dc1-5636-a0ae-fd45747b8bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132649" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{(y-2x)\\,{e}^{x/y}}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,{e}^{x/y}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{x/y}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is defined only when $y>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ge 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,y>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b009a9e-a9d7-5def-b9bb-ebc5fb5d1dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266521} {:answer/id #uuid "962d201e-a8fc-5972-be20-a938590357e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267485} {:answer/id #uuid "afc9083e-741c-58f4-a499-75f94823c48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267726} {:answer/id #uuid "b048f672-dbae-51e2-9ea4-4344c23ab492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267732} {:answer/id #uuid "b5070f65-6ca7-5e11-9fd7-88e61669421d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x<0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962d201e-a8fc-5972-be20-a938590357e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,\\,y\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267485} :answer-field/id #uuid "81ab3ff0-3a57-5d4d-a946-1c1396e93ff6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c09835a-efb6-5fd7-a6fa-37a21ebe5b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-132651" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{y}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2y\\sqrt{x}}$ is defined only when $x>0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${f}_{y}(x,y)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,y\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x>0,y\\ne 0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc6ddd1b-3048-5d1f-91e7-875fe340b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381057} {:answer/id #uuid "5d334c11-359b-5ce5-9c2a-7d356fcb8b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381058} {:answer/id #uuid "17df3516-5fc1-571f-9361-95b28863e220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381059} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5f02b0-9995-5f27-85f7-af62c806fbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381060} {:answer/id #uuid "b32ecd61-4e1d-5b08-87a6-155a284ee4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5f02b0-9995-5f27-85f7-af62c806fbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381060} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b80ece-2d88-5c65-908f-57fe97248a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "25283dae-901a-5291-a7bb-9324644a9dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-132732" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}?$\n\n- The partial derivative with respect to $x$ is defined at $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{x{sin}^{2}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0,1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{z}$ is also defined at $(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\frac{(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{(1){sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124465 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8702032e-0795-5b51-9cc9-f89b35b64fa0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where a Partial Derivative Exists"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abfc7184-dccb-586e-ab1b-1c9431a9abb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\ge 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122535} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe67180-e86f-5591-95d7-99ed5f254084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} < 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122536} {:answer/id #uuid "fc72d45d-bf3e-59de-8294-8bb44ab6a05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} > 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122537} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9d1402-4ede-5a01-8b4c-d4fb2491c85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x, y): x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122538} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddf2b66-7c25-529d-b5d3-07d081df9d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f_{x}$ exists everywhere on the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122539}] :answer-field/id #uuid "56a53f43-74b3-548a-b77c-4de387de6624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122534}] :question/id #uuid "26c20fad-1100-518f-a9ac-5c834fe02e54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36481" :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y) = \\sqrt{1 - x^{2} - y^{2}}$, find the set of values $(x, y)$ on which $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ exists."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323e1e26-806d-5103-9c5b-05be999f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} {:answer/id #uuid "3f71ffa0-c375-5e54-a4b4-82c47e1862fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426705} {:answer/id #uuid "58a495bb-1535-5c0c-8153-9cddc1a1d72c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz\\ge 0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426707} {:answer/id #uuid "66b51739-6d9c-59ee-81d0-4f21e588a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426708} {:answer/id #uuid "ab77e5e6-52f4-5889-9394-0781e3a0ecd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,xyz>0\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "323e1e26-806d-5103-9c5b-05be999f86f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6476017e-359c-522d-8dec-b6d7cd0832c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f23029a5-0003-5b31-a62a-a58e0c29de2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-36527" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{xz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y,z)$ on which all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(xyz)$ is defined only when $-1\\le xyz\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{yz}{\\sqrt{1-(xyz)^{2}}}$ is defined only when \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-(xyz)^{2} & >0 \\\\ (xyz)^{2} & <1 \\\\ |xyz| & <1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives $-1<xyz<1.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domains of ${f}_{y}$ and ${f}_{z}$ are the same:\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={D}_{{f}_{z}}=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivatives of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{z}} & ={D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le xyz\\le 1\\}\\cap \\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1<xyz<1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d023b1a-e54c-5a09-986e-c64e8ea0ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333981} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aad436-bdd3-559d-8993-f9ec2e12126f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334588} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e883eb-0fef-5f4c-9a6c-403643063014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334661} {:answer/id #uuid "d284e0b7-af0b-5d1a-99e5-eded90f88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334828} {:answer/id #uuid "f6908825-368b-5836-af84-e8c9e08903f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d284e0b7-af0b-5d1a-99e5-eded90f88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c726d8d6-8230-5663-b6a4-988732e72c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n- ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409541} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff6ca25-dc52-5f06-b4e5-fccf5f8c424e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409542} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5e8d7a-13cb-5c4a-af4c-8873999183d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409544} {:answer/id #uuid "98963520-6ef4-5229-b4a2-12278d12f050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409549} {:answer/id #uuid "feeb48aa-dc46-5157-aa75-f72619f1adb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dbd6ccf-ed91-536c-bc41-cccc05325c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409541} :answer-field/id #uuid "90260639-fefe-562c-8509-7dc58803f6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538fdcf0-8dcd-525c-becd-0317742dbf1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72010" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}?$\n\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,-1)$\n- ${f}_{y}(-1,-1)$ is defined\n- ${f}_{x}(0,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}\\cdot \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{\\sqrt{xy}}}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(1)(-1)}}=-{e}^{\\sqrt{-1}}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have a negative number under the radical.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(-1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(-1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{1}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,-1) & =\\frac{(-1){e}^{\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{(-1)(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1}}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(0,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}}{2\\sqrt{0\\cdot 1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II  only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e570ff9-ba40-51a6-b10f-3e8b4b8a1efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382216} {:answer/id #uuid "96ff9ddf-e7f3-5cb7-a289-9f327d912d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382217} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4439ab-e45c-5c5c-a37e-64c3b1f4c2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382218} {:answer/id #uuid "4eab8f87-78e7-5b92-8311-4b200cac830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382219} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ea48c5-1f06-568b-b1c8-d61be82ec911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eab8f87-78e7-5b92-8311-4b200cac830b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382219} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c517d13-4ce6-527a-b3ce-ca2edcfc9904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa8a43bb-df08-5eee-88ea-f9abcdd2fb8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72011" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}?$\n\n- ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ is defined\n- Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,0)$\n- ${f}_{x}(1,1)$ is defined" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}\\cdot \\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{x-y}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x-y}}}{2\\sqrt{x-y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,-1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,-1)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}={e}^{\\sqrt{2}},\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{y}$ is also defined at $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,-1)=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}}{2\\sqrt{1-(-1)}}=-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{2}}}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,0)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,0)={e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{e}{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0) & =-\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-0}}}{2\\sqrt{1-0}}=-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{2\\sqrt{1}}=-\\frac{e}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into ${f}_{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(1,1)=\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{1-1}}}{2\\sqrt{1-1}}=\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{0}}=\\frac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II  only'."}]}
